From 397282e5d74b551cf5e3d84132a8eb07f4bae5ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anselm Busse <anselm.busse@outlook.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 07:27:58 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] x86: restore K1OM support removed in 2.39

Binutils 2.39 dropped the L1OM/K1OM sub-architectures (801a7eab11e,
e961c696dcb, c085ab00c7b).  Bring back the K1OM half: bfd_arch_k1om and
cpu-k1om.c, the elf64-k1om and elf64-k1om-freebsd vectors, the elf_k1om
and elf_k1om_fbsd emulations, -march=k1om and CpuK1OM in gas, and the
tests, as they were in 2.38.  L1OM stays removed.  -march=k1om takes the
CPU_UNKNOWN set (all but IAMCU), which is what "unknown" meant for K1OM
before; i386-gen's dependency table cannot express that as a K1OM entry.

As x86-64 now does, elf64-k1om sets ELF_OSABI to ELFOSABI_GNU (without
ELF_OSABI_EXACT), so that IFUNC and the other GNU features keep working,
and elf64-k1om-freebsd matches ELFOSABI_FREEBSD exactly.  K1OM is added
as a secondary target where x86 targets still list secondary targets;
the Solaris and FreeBSD lists that were reorganized upstream get none.

The K1OM vectors follow the Solaris one, which uses 1MB pages since
2.46, so they set ELF_MAXPAGESIZE back to 4KB.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Anselm Busse <anselm.busse@outlook.com>
